(window.DOM_TESTS=window.DOM_TESTS||{})["circulation-3"]={"lecture":"circulation-3","chapters":["gravity","shock","heart-failure"],"questions":[{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b9a07568","chapter":"gravity","section":"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above","type":"calculation","stem":"A vessel is 50 cm below heart level in an upright patient. Approximately how much hydrostatic pressure does gravity add?","options":{"A":"About 19 mmHg.","B":"About 39 mmHg.","C":"About 77 mmHg.","D":"About 100 mmHg."},"answer":"B","why":"Hydrostatic pressure changes by about 0.77 mmHg per centimetre of height; a 50-cm drop adds approximately 39 mmHg.","not":{"A":"Nineteen mmHg corresponds to roughly 25 cm.","C":"Seventy-seven mmHg corresponds to roughly 100 cm.","D":"One hundred mmHg is much larger than the calculated gradient."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:90c745fa","chapter":"gravity","section":"1-gravity-adds-pressure-below-the-heart-and-takes-it-away-above","type":"mechanism","stem":"An ankle and heart-level arterial pressure are measured simultaneously while a patient stands. Which difference should gravity produce?","options":{"A":"Ankle pressure is lower because blood loses energy downward.","B":"Ankle pressure is higher because of the vertical blood column.","C":"Both pressures are equal because cardiac output is shared.","D":"Heart-level pressure is higher because it lies nearer the pump."},"answer":"B","why":"Gravity adds hydrostatic pressure to blood columns below heart level, so ankle arterial pressure exceeds heart-level pressure even before reflex compensation.","not":{"A":"The hydrostatic effect below the heart adds pressure rather than removing it.","C":"Equal cardiac output does not eliminate pressure differences from vertical height.","D":"Pump proximity does not reverse the effect of blood-column weight."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:09724a8f","chapter":"gravity","section":"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation","type":"mechanism","stem":"A student stands abruptly. What links pooling in the leg veins to a lower stroke volume?","options":{"A":"Reduced venous return lowers end-diastolic volume.","B":"Increased afterload raises end-diastolic volume.","C":"Higher leg pressure directly raises coronary flow.","D":"Reduced venous pressure increases ventricular filling."},"answer":"A","why":"Blood pooling lowers venous return and ventricular filling; Frank–Starling physiology then reduces stroke volume.","not":{"B":"Afterload is not the primary cause of reduced initial filling.","C":"Leg pressure does not directly explain the fall in stroke volume.","D":"Venous return falls, rather than increasing, after pooling."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:7544c50d","chapter":"gravity","section":"2-what-standing-up-does-to-the-circulation","type":"mechanism","stem":"A nurse notices ankle swelling after a long standing shift. Which immediate local change promotes this swelling?","options":{"A":"Reduced capillary pressure decreases net filtration.","B":"Raised capillary pressure increases outward filtration.","C":"Increased plasma oncotic pressure draws fluid outward.","D":"Lower interstitial pressure stops all capillary exchange."},"answer":"B","why":"The increased hydrostatic pressure in dependent leg capillaries pushes more fluid into the interstitium and promotes oedema.","not":{"A":"A lower capillary pressure would oppose outward filtration.","C":"Plasma oncotic pressure ordinarily favours fluid retention within blood.","D":"Standing promotes dependent filtration; it does not stop capillary exchange."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:eb61b7ba","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which reflex pattern helps restore arterial pressure during the first seconds of standing?","options":{"A":"More baroreceptor firing and less sympathetic activity.","B":"More baroreceptor firing and more vagal activity.","C":"Less baroreceptor firing and more sympathetic activity.","D":"Less baroreceptor firing and more vagal activity."},"answer":"C","why":"Falling arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, which increases sympathetic drive and reduces vagal influence; heart rate and vascular tone then increase to defend blood pressure.","not":{"A":"Pressure falls, so baroreceptor firing does not increase.","B":"Greater vagal activity would slow the heart.","D":"Vagal activity falls rather than rising during compensation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5e0dc88a","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"A person lying still stands up, then begins walking. What feature makes the calf-muscle pump effective?","options":{"A":"Deep vein compression with one-way valves.","B":"Arterial compression with bidirectional valves.","C":"Capillary contraction during each footstep.","D":"Venule dilation without pressure gradients."},"answer":"A","why":"Calf contractions compress deep veins, while competent venous valves favour upward flow toward the heart instead of backflow.","not":{"B":"Arteries do not rely on venous-style one-way valves.","C":"Capillaries do not function as contractile muscle pumps.","D":"Venous return requires directed pressure gradients, not dilation alone."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:cd3de968","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"During standing, head-level arterial pressure falls by 30 mmHg and cerebral venous pressure falls by 25 mmHg. How does arterial minus venous driving pressure change?","options":{"A":"It falls by about 5 mmHg.","B":"It rises by about 5 mmHg.","C":"It falls by about 25 mmHg.","D":"It rises by about 55 mmHg."},"answer":"A","why":"The arterial–venous difference changes by −30 minus −25, or −5 mmHg. Falling venous and intracranial pressures therefore help protect cerebral perfusion.","not":{"B":"The difference becomes slightly smaller, not larger.","C":"That ignores the fall in cerebral venous pressure.","D":"The changes cannot be added as two rises."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:0d57348b","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"mechanism","stem":"If cerebral oxygen delivery falls during upright posture, which local response can limit the reduction in flow?","options":{"A":"Reduced CO₂ causes arteriolar vasodilatation.","B":"Increased CO₂ causes arteriolar vasoconstriction.","C":"Increased O₂ causes cerebral arteriolar spasm.","D":"High CO₂ and low O₂ promote arteriolar dilation."},"answer":"D","why":"A rise in local carbon dioxide and a fall in oxygen encourage cerebral arteriolar dilation, partly protecting perfusion.","not":{"A":"Reduced CO₂ tends to constrict cerebral vessels.","B":"Increased CO₂ stimulates dilation, not constriction.","C":"When cerebral oxygen delivery is threatened, local oxygen levels fall rather than rise."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:188bea17","chapter":"gravity","section":"3-how-the-body-rescues-blood-pressure","type":"time-course","stem":"Which response becomes particularly important for supporting pressure during sustained rather than momentary upright posture?","options":{"A":"Suppression of aldosterone-driven sodium retention.","B":"Renin–angiotensin effects and renal salt retention.","C":"Loss of sympathetic arteriolar responsiveness.","D":"Persistent inhibition of all vascular reflexes."},"answer":"B","why":"The rapid baroreflex stabilizes pressure within seconds, while the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system supports longer-term pressure and volume.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone supports sodium retention rather than its suppression.","C":"Loss of arteriolar responsiveness would worsen orthostatic control.","D":"Baroreflexes remain available during sustained standing."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:cd2166f2","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"application","stem":"A student faints after standing motionless on parade. Why is a horizontal position helpful?","options":{"A":"It raises circulating red-cell concentration immediately.","B":"It stimulates the skeletal-muscle pump without movement.","C":"It brings the brain nearer heart level, improving flow.","D":"It permanently removes peripheral venous compliance."},"answer":"C","why":"Lying flat shortens the vertical blood column between heart and brain, promptly improving the cerebral pressure available for flow.","not":{"A":"Red-cell concentration does not immediately rise from lying down.","B":"A motionless horizontal position is not itself a muscular contraction.","D":"Veins remain compliant even after the person is laid flat."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:c7d6b36c","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"comparison","stem":"A patient feels faint after intense fear, with bradycardia and marked vasodilatation. Which reflex pattern best explains it?","options":{"A":"Vasovagal sympathetic withdrawal with increased vagal activity.","B":"Baroreflex sympathetic activation with decreased vagal activity.","C":"Cushing response with severe systemic arteriolar constriction.","D":"Bainbridge reflex with accelerated sinus-node firing."},"answer":"A","why":"Vasovagal syncope combines loss of sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone with increased cardiac vagal activity, producing hypotension and bradycardia.","not":{"B":"Normal compensation to falling BP increases sympathetic activity.","C":"The Cushing response usually produces hypertension with bradycardia.","D":"The Bainbridge reflex is associated with increased heart rate during atrial stretch."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:d33d9a0c","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"application","stem":"A tight collar causes transient loss of consciousness in a patient with carotid sinus hypersensitivity. What initiated the fall in pressure?","options":{"A":"A rise in renal aldosterone release.","B":"A direct increase in ventricular contractility.","C":"A sudden increase in circulating blood volume.","D":"Reflex bradycardia with vasodilatation."},"answer":"D","why":"Mechanical carotid sinus stimulation can mimic high arterial stretch, causing excessive vagal slowing and reduced sympathetic vascular tone.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone is not the immediate cause of this pressure fall.","B":"Increased ventricular contractility would tend to defend output.","C":"Circulating blood volume does not suddenly expand with collar pressure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:11d11ef9","chapter":"gravity","section":"4-when-compensation-fails-prolonged-standing-and-fainting","type":"application","stem":"An older patient faints shortly after standing to urinate at night. Which phenomenon does this episode most closely illustrate?","options":{"A":"Carotid sinus syncope caused by neck pressure.","B":"Micturition syncope linked to reflex hypotension.","C":"Septic shock due to widespread inflammatory vasodilatation.","D":"Positive-g blackout from head-level underperfusion."},"answer":"B","why":"Micturition syncope is fainting associated with urination, often after rising at night, due to a reflex pressure fall.","not":{"A":"Carotid sinus syncope requires stimulation of a sensitive carotid region.","C":"There is no evidence of infection-related shock in the stem.","D":"Normal standing to urinate is not high positive acceleration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e2b5d69c","chapter":"gravity","section":"5-orthostatic-postural-hypotension","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"After three minutes upright, pressure changes from 126/82 to 104/77 mmHg. How is this classified using the chapter criterion?","options":{"A":"No orthostatic hypotension because diastolic pressure fell only 5 mmHg.","B":"No orthostatic hypotension because final systolic pressure exceeds 100 mmHg.","C":"Orthostatic hypotension because systolic pressure fell 22 mmHg.","D":"Orthostatic hypotension only if both pressures fall by 20 mmHg."},"answer":"C","why":"A sustained systolic fall of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic fall of at least 10 mmHg within three minutes satisfies the criterion.","not":{"A":"The systolic threshold is met even when the diastolic threshold is not.","B":"The diagnostic threshold concerns the change, not a fixed final pressure.","D":"The definition does not require both components to fall by 20 mmHg."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:ea7d6a98","chapter":"gravity","section":"5-orthostatic-postural-hypotension","type":"application","stem":"A patient becomes dizzy on standing after starting a sympatholytic drug. Which compensatory response is most directly weakened?","options":{"A":"Constriction of systemic arterioles and veins.","B":"Dilatation of cerebral vessels by carbon dioxide.","C":"Contraction of calf muscles during walking.","D":"Flow of blood through competent venous valves."},"answer":"A","why":"Sympatholytic drugs blunt reflex arteriolar and venous constriction, so the circulation cannot adequately oppose blood pooling and falling arterial pressure on standing.","not":{"B":"Cerebral metabolic vasodilatation is not directly a sympathetic vasoconstrictor response.","C":"Walking uses a mechanical muscle pump, not this autonomic drug pathway.","D":"Venous valve directionality is mechanical and not directly abolished by sympatholytic drugs."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:8405c67c","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"application","stem":"Why can vision fail before consciousness during strong positive acceleration?","options":{"A":"The retina receives blood at higher pressure than the heart.","B":"Positive acceleration shifts blood toward the head.","C":"Intracranial pressure exceeds local arterial pressure.","D":"Retinal perfusion falls as blood shifts toward the feet."},"answer":"D","why":"Positive g causes leg pooling and reduced pressure at eye level, so retinal perfusion can fail before cerebral consciousness.","not":{"A":"Pressure at eye level decreases during positive g.","B":"Headward blood movement occurs during negative g.","C":"The mechanism is reduced eye-level arterial perfusion, not an inevitable ICP excess."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e779c3c9","chapter":"gravity","section":"6-acceleration-when-gravity-gets-stronger","type":"application","stem":"Why does positioning an accelerating pilot more horizontally improve tolerance to positive g?","options":{"A":"It increases the distance between the heart and brain.","B":"It directs pooled blood toward the ankles.","C":"It raises the capillary filtration rate in the legs.","D":"It shortens the heart-to-brain hydrostatic column."},"answer":"D","why":"A more horizontal posture decreases the vertical distance over which acceleration reduces pressure toward the head and brain.","not":{"A":"Increasing the vertical distance would worsen the gradient.","B":"Enhanced ankle pooling would reduce central venous return.","C":"Greater dependent filtration would not protect brain perfusion."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:0c0348a7","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"conceptual","stem":"A bleeding patient has a nearly normal arterial pressure but cold skin and reduced urine output. What is the best conclusion?","options":{"A":"Normal pressure excludes significant shock.","B":"Low perfusion can coexist with normal pressure.","C":"Cardiac output must be above its pre-bleeding level.","D":"Blood flow to all organs must remain normal."},"answer":"B","why":"Sympathetic arteriolar constriction can maintain arterial pressure despite reduced cardiac output and poor perfusion of some tissues.","not":{"A":"Shock is defined by inadequate tissue perfusion, not one pressure value.","C":"Haemorrhage reduces venous return and usually lowers output.","D":"Flow can be redistributed away from skin and kidneys."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6d131166","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"classification","stem":"A patient develops low-output shock immediately after a large myocardial infarction without major fluid loss. Which type of shock is most likely?","options":{"A":"Hypovolaemic shock.","B":"Distributive shock.","C":"Cardiogenic shock.","D":"Obstructive shock."},"answer":"C","why":"The damaged heart cannot pump enough blood to meet tissue needs, producing cardiogenic shock despite remaining blood volume.","not":{"A":"Hypovolaemia requires a major loss of circulating volume.","B":"Distributive shock primarily reflects systemic vasodilatation.","D":"Obstructive shock results from a physical block to filling or flow."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:7cf076e8","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"classification","stem":"After a massive pulmonary embolus, blood cannot pass normally through the pulmonary arteries. Which category best explains the collapse?","options":{"A":"Obstructive shock.","B":"Cardiogenic shock.","C":"Distributive shock.","D":"Hypovolaemic shock."},"answer":"A","why":"A large embolus physically obstructs pulmonary blood flow, reducing effective forward circulation and producing obstructive shock.","not":{"B":"Cardiogenic shock chiefly reflects primary pump dysfunction.","C":"Distributive shock involves loss of systemic vascular tone.","D":"Hypovolaemic shock requires loss of blood or other fluid."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6c708177","chapter":"shock","section":"1-what-is-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient has severe hypotension despite normal blood volume, after widespread relaxation of resistance and capacitance vessels. What is the main defect?","options":{"A":"Red-cell loss with a normal vascular capacity.","B":"Excessive arteriolar constriction and raised resistance.","C":"Isolated myocardial failure without vascular changes.","D":"Low vascular resistance with circulating blood pooling."},"answer":"D","why":"In distributive shock, widespread vasodilatation expands vascular capacity and lowers effective filling and peripheral resistance.","not":{"A":"The stem excludes a major blood volume deficit.","B":"The primary change is dilation rather than constriction.","C":"A primary pump defect would define cardiogenic shock."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6579b0c7","chapter":"shock","section":"2-haemorrhage-the-model-for-all-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which chain best describes the early circulatory consequences of severe haemorrhage?","options":{"A":"Reduced blood volume → reduced venous return → reduced stroke volume.","B":"Reduced blood volume → increased venous return → reduced stroke volume.","C":"Reduced blood volume → increased ventricular filling → increased output.","D":"Reduced blood volume → increased plasma volume → increased output."},"answer":"A","why":"Blood loss reduces central venous filling, lowering end-diastolic volume, stroke volume and cardiac output.","not":{"B":"Venous return falls rather than rises after blood loss.","C":"Ventricular filling decreases with reduced circulating volume.","D":"Haemorrhage does not immediately expand plasma volume."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:8ac72fe1","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is the pulse often fast but weak in compensated hypovolaemic shock?","options":{"A":"Reduced sympathetic drive combines with increased stroke volume.","B":"Increased vagal drive combines with reduced afterload.","C":"Increased sympathetic drive combines with reduced stroke volume.","D":"Reduced vagal drive combines with increased ventricular filling."},"answer":"C","why":"Baroreceptor unloading raises sympathetic activity and heart rate, while low filling produces a small stroke volume and weak pulse.","not":{"A":"Sympathetic drive rises, not falls, during compensation.","B":"Vagal influence generally decreases, not increases.","D":"Ventricular filling is reduced after haemorrhage."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:88f71989","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient in hypovolaemic shock has very little urine output. Which change directly contributes to this sign?","options":{"A":"Increased GFR following higher renal flow.","B":"Reduced GFR following renal vasoconstriction.","C":"Increased urine flow through higher arterial pressure.","D":"Reduced tubular sodium uptake from low aldosterone."},"answer":"B","why":"Low perfusion and sympathetic renal vasoconstriction decrease glomerular filtration, while hormonal compensation promotes sodium and water retention.","not":{"A":"Renal flow and filtration fall rather than rise.","C":"Shock lowers rather than raises effective renal perfusion.","D":"Aldosterone is activated and promotes sodium retention."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:f1eda6be","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A severely underperfused patient begins breathing rapidly, although the lungs were initially healthy. What drives this change?","options":{"A":"Lactic acidosis stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors.","B":"Increased aerobic ATP production stimulates breathing.","C":"High tissue oxygen content increases respiratory drive.","D":"Suppression of the peripheral chemoreceptors stimulates breathing."},"answer":"A","why":"Inadequate tissue oxygen delivery promotes anaerobic metabolism and lactic acidosis. The resulting acidemia can stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors and increase ventilation; reduced oxygen delivery alone is not equivalent to low arterial oxygen tension.","not":{"B":"Aerobic metabolism is limited by inadequate oxygen delivery.","C":"Excess oxygen is not the initiating problem in shock.","D":"Chemoreceptor stimulation, rather than suppression, promotes respiration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b4be6045","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with acute blood loss develops intense thirst. Which hormonal changes best explain the response?","options":{"A":"Lower renin and lower angiotensin II.","B":"Higher aldosterone and reduced vasopressin.","C":"Higher angiotensin II and higher vasopressin.","D":"Lower vasopressin and greater water excretion."},"answer":"C","why":"Reduced effective circulating volume activates renin–angiotensin signalling and ADH, increasing thirst and promoting water conservation.","not":{"A":"Renin and angiotensin II increase during volume depletion.","B":"Vasopressin rises rather than falls in hypovolaemia.","D":"The kidneys tend to retain rather than excrete water."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:39858957","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"application","stem":"A previously anxious patient with severe prolonged shock becomes apathetic and poorly responsive. Which explanation is most concerning?","options":{"A":"Red-cell production has rapidly restored oxygen delivery.","B":"Skin vasoconstriction has resolved the shock.","C":"The skeletal-muscle pump has become overactive.","D":"Worsening cerebral ischaemia impairs brain function."},"answer":"D","why":"Restlessness can accompany early sympathetic arousal; later apathy may indicate worsening cerebral ischaemia and failing perfusion.","not":{"A":"Erythropoiesis cannot rapidly reverse acute circulatory collapse.","B":"An improvement in perfusion would not account for deterioration.","C":"The muscle pump does not explain progressive depressed consciousness."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:ffdaffb3","chapter":"shock","section":"3-what-you-see-and-why","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why does persistent lactic acidosis make circulatory shock harder to reverse?","options":{"A":"It increases ventricular force and vascular responsiveness.","B":"It depresses contraction and catecholamine responses.","C":"It directly restores aerobic ATP production.","D":"It prevents further fluid filtration from capillaries."},"answer":"B","why":"Acidosis from anaerobic metabolism weakens cardiac pumping and reduces vascular response to catecholamines, undermining compensation.","not":{"A":"Acidosis impairs rather than strengthens these functions.","C":"Aerobic ATP generation still requires oxygen.","D":"Acidosis does not reliably prevent fluid movement into tissues."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:08ed4a56","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"During haemorrhage, why are the brain and heart relatively protected while skin and renal flow decrease?","options":{"A":"Skin and renal arterioles constrict more strongly.","B":"Coronary veins release enough blood to replace all loss.","C":"Cerebral arterioles constrict more than skin arterioles.","D":"Kidney vessels lose their sympathetic innervation."},"answer":"A","why":"Sympathetic constriction is pronounced in skin, kidneys and splanchnic beds, redistributing limited flow toward critical organs.","not":{"B":"Venous reserves do not replace an entire major haemorrhage.","C":"Severe cerebral constriction would not protect brain flow.","D":"Renal sympathetic activity increases rather than disappears."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:f8eb26cd","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"What is the immediate benefit of sympathetic constriction in splanchnic and pulmonary venous reservoirs during blood loss?","options":{"A":"Increased peripheral pooling of the remaining blood.","B":"Rapid production of new erythrocytes in venous blood.","C":"Mobilisation of stored blood toward the heart.","D":"Reduction in ventricular filling during compensation."},"answer":"C","why":"Venoconstriction expels part of the blood stored in compliant veins, improving central return and supporting ventricular filling.","not":{"A":"Pooling would worsen central volume depletion.","B":"Red-cell production takes weeks rather than seconds.","D":"The mechanism supports rather than reduces central filling."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:5bf40867","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Which endocrine combination helps sustain arterial pressure during acute haemorrhage?","options":{"A":"Reduced adrenal catecholamines with reduced renin.","B":"Increased aldosterone with suppressed angiotensin II.","C":"Reduced ADH with increased sodium excretion.","D":"More catecholamines and renin–angiotensin activity."},"answer":"D","why":"The adrenal medulla releases catecholamines while RAAS promotes vasoconstriction and volume conservation after blood loss.","not":{"A":"The adrenal medulla and renin system are activated.","B":"Aldosterone release follows increased angiotensin signalling.","C":"ADH rises and renal water loss is reduced."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6782e4e4","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient in shock has reduced systemic capillary hydrostatic pressure. What useful change in fluid movement can follow?","options":{"A":"More filtration from capillaries into interstitial tissue.","B":"Movement of interstitial fluid back into plasma.","C":"Immediate generation of red blood cells within capillaries.","D":"Rapid transfer of plasma proteins into all cells."},"answer":"B","why":"Reduced capillary hydrostatic pressure decreases outward filtration and can favour transient movement of interstitial fluid into plasma during hypovolaemia, helping restore circulating volume.","not":{"A":"Lower capillary pressure tends to oppose outward filtration.","C":"Fluid shifts do not directly create new erythrocytes.","D":"Protein entry into cells is not the compensatory mechanism."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:90fcd341","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why does vasopressin release help limit further circulatory deterioration after a large bleed?","options":{"A":"It conserves renal water and constricts some vessels.","B":"It increases renal free-water loss and arterial dilation.","C":"It directly restores depleted red-cell mass.","D":"It suppresses the renal response to low blood volume."},"answer":"A","why":"Vasopressin promotes water reabsorption and can raise vascular tone, supporting circulating volume and arterial pressure during hypovolaemia.","not":{"B":"These effects are opposite to vasopressin's actions.","C":"Erythropoietin, not ADH, supports later red-cell replacement.","D":"Volume depletion normally stimulates the vasopressin response."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:2a1a2f20","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"Immediately after rapid loss of a large volume of whole blood, haemoglobin concentration is unexpectedly unchanged. Why?","options":{"A":"Red-cell mass increases enough to compensate.","B":"Plasma enters faster than red cells are lost.","C":"Red cells and plasma were lost together.","D":"Erythropoietin instantly makes new red cells."},"answer":"C","why":"Acute whole-blood loss removes red cells and plasma proportionately, so concentration may remain unchanged before fluid replacement.","not":{"A":"Red-cell mass falls with whole-blood loss.","B":"Early dilution has not yet occurred in this scenario.","D":"Erythropoiesis takes days to weeks, not minutes."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:bf41b20d","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"time-course","stem":"After bleeding stops, both haemoglobin and plasma-protein concentrations fall as circulating volume recovers. What explains this?","options":{"A":"Added fluid dilutes the remaining blood components.","B":"New red-cell production lowers haemoglobin concentration.","C":"Raised renal filtration removes most circulating proteins.","D":"Venoconstriction selectively removes haemoglobin from blood."},"answer":"A","why":"Fluid recruited from tissues or given intravenously restores volume before red-cell and protein mass recover, producing dilution.","not":{"B":"New red-cell production raises rather than lowers haemoglobin mass.","C":"Renal filtration is generally depressed during acute severe shock.","D":"Venoconstriction redistributes blood rather than removing haemoglobin."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6bb17301","chapter":"shock","section":"4-how-the-body-fights-back","type":"time-course","stem":"Which time course of spontaneous recovery best fits the lecture after bleeding has been stopped?","options":{"A":"Blood volume in weeks; haemoglobin in hours.","B":"Both volume and haemoglobin fully recover in minutes.","C":"Haemoglobin in 12–72 hours; volume in 4–5 weeks.","D":"Volume in 12–72 hours; haemoglobin in 4–5 weeks."},"answer":"D","why":"Tissue-fluid recruitment and renal retention restore blood volume within roughly 12–72 hours; erythropoietin-driven red-cell recovery requires several weeks.","not":{"A":"The timing of the two processes is reversed.","B":"Neither process replaces major losses within minutes.","C":"Haemoglobin replacement occurs much more slowly than fluid recovery."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:4641fd8e","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"During progressive shock, capillary hydrostatic pressure paradoxically rises while systemic arterial pressure is falling. Which microvascular pattern best explains it?","options":{"A":"Precapillary constriction with freely draining venules.","B":"Precapillary constriction with widened venous outlets.","C":"Increased venular drainage with reduced capillary entry.","D":"Open capillary entry with constricted venules."},"answer":"D","why":"With capillary entry relatively open but venular outflow restricted, blood stagnates and local capillary pressure rises, driving fluid into the interstitium.","not":{"A":"Lower entry with free outflow would not cause the described stasis.","B":"Both reduced entry and improved drainage favour lower capillary pressure.","C":"Greater drainage would reduce rather than increase local stagnation."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b038a52d","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient in severe progressive shock loses the normal increase in sympathetic activity despite a further fall in pressure. What may have failed?","options":{"A":"The skeletal-muscle pump has permanently strengthened.","B":"Ischaemia has depressed the brainstem vasomotor centre.","C":"The kidney has increased glomerular filtration markedly.","D":"The ventricles have become insensitive to vagal input only."},"answer":"B","why":"Severe prolonged cerebral ischaemia can depress the vasomotor centre, causing sympathetic withdrawal, vasodilatation and further hypotension.","not":{"A":"A stronger muscle pump would support rather than abolish perfusion.","C":"Severe shock reduces GFR rather than causing hyperfiltration.","D":"The key failure is central sympathetic control, not isolated vagal resistance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:8268eea4","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"How can impaired coronary perfusion create a vicious cycle during severe shock?","options":{"A":"Coronary ischaemia weakens contraction, further reducing output.","B":"Low coronary blood flow increases myocardial ATP stores.","C":"Myocardial hypoxia lowers arterial resistance enough to compensate.","D":"Reduced stroke volume directly increases coronary perfusion pressure."},"answer":"A","why":"Coronary ischaemia depresses myocardial contractility, lowering output and pressure, which further compromises myocardial blood supply.","not":{"B":"Ischaemia reduces rather than increases available myocardial ATP.","C":"Lower resistance cannot fully resolve a collapsing pump.","D":"Lower cardiac output tends to worsen coronary perfusion, not restore it."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b8cde4d2","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"application","stem":"A patient develops persistent acute kidney injury after prolonged shock. Which tissue injury best accounts for this complication?","options":{"A":"Rapidly reversible isolated renal vasodilatation.","B":"Increased tubular oxygen delivery without injury.","C":"Renal tubular injury after sustained ischaemia.","D":"Direct erythrocyte production within the kidney."},"answer":"C","why":"Sustained renal underperfusion can injure the tubular epithelium, leading to acute kidney injury beyond the initial pressure fall.","not":{"A":"A transient isolated vessel response would not explain persistent injury.","B":"Ischaemia means reduced rather than increased oxygen supply.","D":"Erythropoietin production does not directly replace renal tubules."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:bb6d79a7","chapter":"shock","section":"5-when-shock-feeds-on-itself-progressive-and-irreversible-shock","type":"conceptual","stem":"A patient with massive haemorrhage remains severely hypotensive after blood volume is completely restored. Which stage fits?","options":{"A":"Postural hypotension from remaining upright.","B":"Early compensated hypovolaemic shock with intact reflexes.","C":"Transient vasovagal faint without major tissue damage.","D":"Irreversible shock after extensive tissue injury."},"answer":"D","why":"Severe myocardial, neural and microvascular damage may prevent recovery even after the original blood-volume loss has been corrected.","not":{"A":"Postural hypotension should depend on standing and impaired compensation.","B":"Compensated shock should retain useful mechanisms for pressure support.","C":"An uncomplicated faint generally improves rapidly when lying flat."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:c9341fb2","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"application","stem":"A shocked patient has a femoral fracture with no visible bleeding. Why must hypovolaemia still be considered?","options":{"A":"External bleeding is required to reduce circulating volume.","B":"Major haemorrhage can be concealed within the thigh.","C":"A fractured femur immediately increases plasma volume.","D":"Hidden bleeding causes only distributive shock."},"answer":"B","why":"A fractured femur can conceal a large blood loss in surrounding tissues, causing hypovolaemic shock despite intact skin.","not":{"A":"Internal haemorrhage may occur without external blood.","C":"Bleeding decreases rather than increases circulating volume.","D":"Occult bleeding is a form of volume-loss shock."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e4e897af","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"comparison","stem":"A burn patient presents with shock and an initially high haematocrit. What explains the contrast with recent whole-blood haemorrhage?","options":{"A":"Burns selectively destroy red cells circulating in blood.","B":"Burns cause immediate erythropoietin-driven erythrocytosis.","C":"Plasma leakage leaves intravascular red cells concentrated.","D":"Burns produce increased circulating volume despite shock."},"answer":"C","why":"Severe burns can cause plasma to leak through damaged skin while red cells remain, producing haemoconcentration and raised haematocrit.","not":{"A":"Selective red-cell destruction would tend to lower red-cell concentration.","B":"Erythropoiesis is much too slow to explain this early result.","D":"Plasma loss reduces rather than increases effective circulating volume."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:8ee2ff45","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Crushed skeletal muscle releases a pigment associated with renal tubular injury. Which substance is implicated?","options":{"A":"Myoglobin.","B":"Albumin.","C":"Bilirubin.","D":"Fibrinogen."},"answer":"A","why":"Myoglobin released by damaged skeletal muscle can damage renal tubules, worsening renal failure in traumatic shock.","not":{"B":"Albumin is a circulating plasma protein, not the muscle pigment.","C":"Bilirubin is associated with haem breakdown rather than muscle pigment.","D":"Fibrinogen participates in coagulation, not the tubular pigment injury described."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:f98e475d","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with severe bacterial infection has systemic vasodilatation and capillary leak. What type of shock is illustrated?","options":{"A":"Obstructive shock from impaired forward flow.","B":"Cardiogenic shock from isolated muscle failure.","C":"Hypovolaemic shock from proportional blood loss.","D":"Distributive shock from vascular dilatation."},"answer":"D","why":"Bacterial toxins and inflammatory mediators can markedly dilate blood vessels and increase permeability, causing distributive septic shock.","not":{"A":"A mechanical obstruction is not described.","B":"Primary isolated myocardial damage is not the stated mechanism.","C":"Systemic inflammation and vasodilatation are the dominant findings."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:574b57de","chapter":"shock","section":"6-the-other-types-of-shock","type":"mechanism","stem":"Minutes after a severe allergic exposure, a patient's pressure drops with generalised swelling. Which vascular action is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Generalised constriction with reduced capillary permeability.","B":"Histamine-related dilatation with increased capillary permeability.","C":"Selective haemorrhage into the gastrointestinal tract.","D":"Increased myocardial pump function with reduced venous capacitance."},"answer":"B","why":"Anaphylaxis releases mediators including histamine that widen vessels and increase capillary leakage, reducing effective perfusion.","not":{"A":"Anaphylaxis typically causes dilation and increased leak.","C":"Occult bleeding is not the primary allergic vascular mechanism.","D":"Higher pump performance would not explain the sudden widespread leakage."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:f82249a1","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"1-what-is-heart-failure","type":"conceptual","stem":"A patient has near-normal resting cardiac output but very high ventricular filling pressures and pulmonary congestion. Which statement fits best?","options":{"A":"Normal resting output excludes heart failure.","B":"Heart failure requires reduced ejection fraction.","C":"High filling pressure proves adequate pumping.","D":"Heart failure can occur with normal output."},"answer":"D","why":"Heart failure includes states where output is maintained only at abnormally high filling pressures, causing congestion.","not":{"A":"A near-normal output can be maintained at harmful filling pressures.","B":"Diastolic failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction.","C":"High filling pressures can be evidence of dysfunction rather than normality."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:76c51697","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"1-what-is-heart-failure","type":"comparison","stem":"Which contrast best distinguishes forward failure from backward failure?","options":{"A":"Forward is venous congestion; backward is low organ blood flow.","B":"Forward reduces delivery; backward causes venous congestion.","C":"Forward is confined to the right heart; backward to the left.","D":"Forward indicates excess cardiac output; backward low afterload."},"answer":"B","why":"Forward failure describes inadequate delivery to downstream tissues, whereas backward failure describes elevated pressure and congestion upstream of a failing chamber.","not":{"A":"The two effects are reversed in this statement.","C":"Either side can produce both forward and backward consequences.","D":"Reduced flow and congestion, not excessive output, are the defining patterns."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:4ba97fe0","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"comparison","stem":"A stiff ventricle has high filling pressure but a preserved ejection fraction. What is the primary defect?","options":{"A":"Impaired relaxation and filling.","B":"Increased contractility and ejection.","C":"Reduced electrical activation of the SA node.","D":"Excessive ventricular compliance."},"answer":"A","why":"Diastolic failure reflects impaired relaxation and stiff ventricular filling; the fraction of blood ejected can remain normal despite elevated filling pressures and reduced filling volume.","not":{"B":"Increased contractility is not the primary defect.","C":"SA-node dysfunction does not define diastolic heart failure.","D":"A stiff ventricle has reduced, not excessive, compliance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:7cd7388c","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"comparison","stem":"An echocardiogram shows poor ventricular contraction and a reduced ejection fraction. Which primary failure pattern does this support?","options":{"A":"Stiff ventricular filling with preserved fraction.","B":"Increased relaxation with low filling pressure.","C":"Isolated systemic vasodilatation without pump disease.","D":"Systolic dysfunction with impaired ejection."},"answer":"D","why":"Systolic heart failure primarily reflects reduced contractility; a smaller proportion of end-diastolic volume is ejected during systole.","not":{"A":"Stiff filling is the typical description of diastolic failure.","B":"Improved relaxation is not impaired systolic pumping.","C":"Vascular dilation alone does not define ventricular systolic failure."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:f5d79fa4","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"2-systolic-and-diastolic-failure-can-t-squeeze-or-can-t-fill","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why might tachycardia particularly worsen output in a patient with a stiff ventricle?","options":{"A":"It lengthens diastole and reduces filling pressure.","B":"It shortens the available time for impaired diastolic filling.","C":"It directly increases ventricular compliance throughout diastole.","D":"It eliminates the need for atrial contribution to filling."},"answer":"B","why":"A stiff ventricle already fills poorly and needs time; tachycardia shortens diastole and further limits effective ventricular filling.","not":{"A":"Tachycardia shortens rather than lengthens the diastolic interval.","C":"Faster rate does not directly cure ventricular stiffness.","D":"Atrial contribution can remain important in stiff ventricles."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:90ddec4f","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"mechanism","stem":"What directly promotes pulmonary oedema in left-sided backward heart failure?","options":{"A":"Reduced systemic venous pressure throughout the body.","B":"Raised pulmonary venous and capillary pressure.","C":"Reduced pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure.","D":"Raised right atrial pressure without lung congestion."},"answer":"B","why":"Left ventricular failure increases left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures, driving filtration into the lungs.","not":{"A":"Systemic venous pressure is more closely linked to right-sided failure.","C":"Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure would oppose filtration.","D":"Right atrial pressure alone does not explain pulmonary oedema."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1ebb7230","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"application","stem":"A patient with left-sided forward heart failure reports fatigue and produces very little urine. Which mechanism links the two?","options":{"A":"Elevated venous pressure increases renal filtration.","B":"Pulmonary congestion causes systemic arteriolar dilation.","C":"Reduced systemic perfusion limits muscle and renal blood flow.","D":"Increased left ventricular output stimulates renal water loss."},"answer":"C","why":"Reduced blood ejected from the left heart impairs delivery to skeletal muscle and kidneys, causing fatigue and oliguria.","not":{"A":"Higher venous pressure does not directly improve GFR.","B":"The primary forward mechanism is reduced output, not pulmonary congestion.","D":"The syndrome involves reduced rather than excessive systemic delivery."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:52567d94","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"mechanism","stem":"What can follow when the right ventricle fails to deliver adequate forward flow to the lungs?","options":{"A":"Less blood reaches the left heart for systemic output.","B":"Left ventricular filling necessarily increases sharply.","C":"Pulmonary flow rises despite low right ventricular output.","D":"Systemic tissues receive more oxygen-rich blood immediately."},"answer":"A","why":"Reduced right ventricular output limits pulmonary flow and subsequent return to the left atrium, compromising left-sided filling and delivery.","not":{"B":"Less pulmonary venous return tends to decrease left-sided filling.","C":"The right heart cannot deliver excess flow when severely failing.","D":"Reduced pulmonary transit cannot raise systemic oxygen delivery."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1bb5dd68","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"application","stem":"A patient with left-sided heart failure feels breathless when lying flat but improves after sitting up. Why?","options":{"A":"Lying flat reduces central blood volume.","B":"Sitting up increases venous return from the legs.","C":"Lying flat shifts peripheral blood toward congested lungs.","D":"Sitting up directly increases pulmonary capillary pressure."},"answer":"C","why":"Supine positioning returns peripheral blood to the thorax; a failing left ventricle cannot handle the extra flow, increasing pulmonary venous congestion.","not":{"A":"Central thoracic volume commonly increases rather than decreases supine.","B":"Sitting upright tends to reduce thoracic venous return.","D":"A higher pulmonary capillary pressure would worsen breathlessness."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:1914e15a","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"application","stem":"An examination reveals raised JVP, a tender enlarged liver, ascites and ankle oedema. Which haemodynamic change unifies these?","options":{"A":"High systemic venous pressure behind the right ventricle.","B":"Increased pulmonary venous pressure without systemic congestion.","C":"Reduced central venous pressure from depleted blood volume.","D":"Reduced filtration from peripheral capillaries."},"answer":"A","why":"Right ventricular backward failure elevates right atrial and systemic venous pressures, causing jugular distension, hepatic congestion and dependent oedema.","not":{"B":"Isolated pulmonary venous congestion principally reflects left-sided failure.","C":"Right-sided backward failure increases rather than decreases venous pressure.","D":"Higher systemic venous pressure promotes, rather than reduces, filtration."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:e7dd3642","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up","type":"mechanism","stem":"How can chronic left ventricular failure eventually weaken the right ventricle?","options":{"A":"Left ventricular failure directly increases renal erythropoietin.","B":"Pulmonary circulation stops exposing the right heart to pressure.","C":"Right ventricular afterload falls as pulmonary pressure rises.","D":"Pulmonary pressure rises, increasing right ventricular workload."},"answer":"D","why":"Left-sided failure can raise pulmonary vascular pressure, forcing the right ventricle to eject against a higher afterload over time.","not":{"A":"Erythropoietin is not the principal link between the ventricles.","B":"Pulmonary pressure transmission and increased load are central.","C":"Higher pulmonary pressure increases, not decreases, right ventricular afterload."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:7271e108","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can prolonged renin–angiotensin–aldosterone activation worsen heart-failure oedema?","options":{"A":"It causes sustained urinary sodium loss.","B":"It decreases circulating volume and filling pressure.","C":"Salt and water retention raises venous pressure.","D":"It prevents compensatory arterial vasoconstriction."},"answer":"C","why":"RAAS initially supports blood pressure, but prolonged salt and water retention increases filling pressures and oedema.","not":{"A":"Aldosterone promotes sodium retention rather than loss.","B":"RAAS tends to expand, not deplete, extracellular volume.","D":"Angiotensin II supports rather than blocks vasoconstriction."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:76695699","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why might prolonged sympathetic arteriolar constriction worsen systolic heart failure despite initially preserving blood pressure?","options":{"A":"It decreases afterload and reduces ventricular work.","B":"It increases afterload and ventricular workload.","C":"It eliminates the need for renal sodium conservation.","D":"It increases ventricular compliance during each beat."},"answer":"B","why":"Higher arteriolar resistance raises afterload and myocardial work, making ejection harder for the failing heart over time.","not":{"A":"Arteriolar constriction increases rather than decreases resistance.","C":"Sympathetic activation does not eliminate RAAS responses.","D":"Afterload elevation does not directly improve ventricular compliance."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:b4c68362","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"A patient with decompensated heart failure gains more water weight despite established oedema. Which additional hormonal response can worsen this?","options":{"A":"Increased natriuretic peptide-mediated water loss.","B":"Reduced collecting-duct response to vasopressin.","C":"More vasopressin-driven renal water reabsorption.","D":"Suppressed renin activity with higher urinary output."},"answer":"C","why":"Reduced effective arterial filling in heart failure can stimulate vasopressin, increasing renal free-water reabsorption and worsening congestion despite excess total body fluid.","not":{"A":"Increased natriuretic water loss would tend to oppose fluid overload.","B":"A lower collecting-duct response to vasopressin would reduce water retention.","D":"Suppression of renin and increased diuresis would oppose retention."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:80ae5c29","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"In a failing ventricle, extra filling causes a large pressure rise but little improvement in stroke volume. Why?","options":{"A":"The Frank–Starling output curve has become relatively flat.","B":"The ventricle has regained normal contractile reserve.","C":"The venous system no longer receives blood from the organs.","D":"The pulmonary valves begin opening during atrial systole."},"answer":"A","why":"A weak ventricle has limited ability to convert increased end-diastolic filling into extra output, so congestion rises disproportionately.","not":{"B":"Normal contractile reserve would improve output as filling rises.","C":"Venous return continues even when a ventricle pumps poorly.","D":"Valve opening timing is not the cause of the flattened curve."}},{"id":"lt-circulation-3:6c243b6a","chapter":"heart-failure","section":"4-what-does-the-body-do-about-it-and-why-does-that-make-it-worse","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why can chronic haemodynamic stress ultimately worsen myocardial pumping even if some early adaptations support output?","options":{"A":"Chronic stress prevents any structural change to ventricular muscle.","B":"The heart permanently converts itself to skeletal muscle.","C":"Ventricular wall tension disappears as filling pressure rises.","D":"Remodelling causes hypertrophy or dilation with dysfunction."},"answer":"D","why":"A chronically stressed ventricle may undergo hypertrophy or dilation; these structural adaptations can eventually impair efficiency and function.","not":{"A":"Long-term cardiac loading can change ventricular structure.","B":"Cardiac muscle does not become skeletal muscle during remodelling.","C":"Filling pressure and wall stress often increase rather than disappear."}}]};
